applyDualLR -- apply dualLR to a single basis pair (T_nu, T_mu)
***************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        applyDualLR((lambda, mu, nu), Q, {T_nu, T_mu}, n)
        applyDualLR(..., Convention => "Row" | "Filling" | "Weyl")
  * Inputs:
      * (lambda, mu, nu), a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,
      * Q, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, an LR tableau
      * {T_nu, T_mu}, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a 2-element list
        containing the source basis pair (a tableau of shape $\nu$ and a
        tableau of shape $\mu$).  Each may be a List or a Filling, interpreted
        in the natural form of the convention (see *note applyPieri:
        applyPieri,), independently.
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Convention => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value
        "Row"
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, list of pairs {coefficient,
        T_lambda} representing the image in $S_\lambda V$

Description
===========

Note: the {T_nu, T_mu} argument must be a List (not a Sequence), since M2
flattens nested Sequences in function-call arg lists but does not flatten
Lists.  Non-standard inputs are straightened first.
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : shapes = ({2,1}, {1}, {1,1});                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : Q = (lrTableaux shapes)#0;                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : applyDualLR(shapes, Q, {{{0},{1}}, {{0},{}}}, 3)|
|                                                     |
|       1                                             |
|o3 = {{-, {{0, 0}, {1}}}}                            |
|       3                                             |
|                                                     |
|o3 : List                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------+

