dualLR -- GL-equivariant projection S_nu V ⊗ S_mu V --> S_lambda V at a chosen LR tableau
*****************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        dualLR((lambda, mu, nu), Q, n)
        dualLR((lambda, mu, nu), Q, P)
        dualLR(..., Convention => "Row" | "Filling" | "Weyl")
  * Inputs:
      * (lambda, mu, nu), a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,, three
        partitions with $|\lambda| = |\mu| + |\nu|$ and $S_\lambda V$ appearing
        in $S_\nu V \otimes S_\mu V$ (i.e. $c^\lambda_{\mu,\nu} > 0$)
      * Q, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, an LR tableau of shape
        $\lambda/\mu$ with content $\nu$ (as returned by *note lrTableaux:
        lrTableaux,) singling out one of the $c^\lambda_{\mu,\nu}$ copies of
        $S_\lambda V$
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, or pass a polynomial ring whose
        numgens is used
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Convention => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value
        "Row", , basis convention for the three Schur factors.
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a $\mathbb{Q}$-matrix of size
        $\dim S_\lambda V \times (\dim S_\nu V \cdot \dim S_\mu V)$, the
        $GL_n$-equivariant projection onto Q's specific copy of $S_\lambda V$
        inside $S_\nu V \otimes S_\mu V$

Description
===========

Returns the $GL_n$-equivariant projection onto the LR-summand of $S_\nu V
\otimes S_\mu V$ indexed by Q (unique up to scalar by Schur's lemma).  When the
LR coefficient $c^\lambda_{\mu,\nu}$ exceeds 1, distinct LR tableaux Q give
different projections, each onto its own copy of $S_\lambda V$;
$\mathrm{dualLR}_Q \circ \mathrm{lrMap}_{Q'} = 0$ when $Q \neq Q'$, and
$\mathrm{dualLR}_Q \circ \mathrm{lrMap}_Q = \mathrm{Id}$.


Construction: stack the LR inclusions *note lrMap: lrMap, for every $(\lambda',
Q')$ with $c^{\lambda'}_{\mu,\nu} > 0$ into a square invertible matrix on
$S_\nu V \otimes S_\mu V$ (square by the LR/Pieri rule) and read off the rows
of its inverse corresponding to $(\lambda, Q)$.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : shapes = ({2,1}, {1}, {1,1});                                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : Q = (lrTableaux shapes)#0;                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = lrMap(shapes, Q, 3);   -- inclusion S_(2,1) V -> S_(1,1) V ⊗ S_(1) V|
|                                                                             |
|              9       8                                                      |
|o3 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : N = dualLR(shapes, Q, 3);  -- projection back onto Q's copy             |
|                                                                             |
|              8       9                                                      |
|o4 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : N * M == id_(QQ^(numColumns M))                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = true                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : Qs = lrTableaux({3,2,1}, {2,1}, {2,1});  -- multiplicity-2 case |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : M0 = lrMap(({3,2,1},{2,1},{2,1}), Qs#0, 3);                     |
|                                                                     |
|              64       8                                             |
|o7 : Matrix QQ   <-- QQ                                              |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : M1 = lrMap(({3,2,1},{2,1},{2,1}), Qs#1, 3);                     |
|                                                                     |
|              64       8                                             |
|o8 : Matrix QQ   <-- QQ                                              |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : N0 = dualLR(({3,2,1},{2,1},{2,1}), Qs#0, 3);                    |
|                                                                     |
|              8       64                                             |
|o9 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : N0 * M0 == id_(QQ^(numColumns M0))   -- recovers Q_0's copy    |
|                                                                     |
|o10 = true                                                           |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : N0 * M1 == 0                          -- annihilates Q_1's copy|
|                                                                     |
|o11 = true                                                           |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note lrMap: lrMap, -- GL(V)-equivariant Littlewood-Richardson inclusion
  * *note lrTableaux: lrTableaux, -- enumerate Littlewood-Richardson tableaux
  * *note applyDualLR: applyDualLR, -- apply dualLR to a single basis pair
    (T_nu, T_mu)
  * *note dualPieri: dualPieri, -- GL-equivariant projection Sym^d V ⊗ S_lambda
    V --> S_mu V
  * *note verifyEquivariant: verifyEquivariant, -- rigorously verify
    GL_n-equivariance of a Schur-rep matrix
  * *note Convention: Convention, -- tableau basis convention used by
    PieriMaps' overhauled functions

