dualPieriColumn -- GL-equivariant projection wedge^d V ⊗ S_lambda V --> S_mu V
******************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        dualPieriColumn(mu, cols, P)
        dualPieriColumn(..., Convention => "Row" | "Filling" | "Weyl")
  * Inputs:
      * mu, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, the target shape: $\mu = \lambda
        + (\text{vertical $d$-strip})$ with $d = \#\mathrm{cols}$
      * cols, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of column indices
        recording the strip $\mu/\lambda$
      * P, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, a
        SkewCommutative ring ($\wedge^d V$ is encoded there)
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Convention => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value
        "Filling"
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a $K$-matrix of size $\dim S_\mu
        V \times \bigl(\dim S_\lambda V \cdot \binom{n}{d}\bigr)$.  Columns
        indexed by $(T_\lambda, \alpha)$ pairs ($\alpha$ an exterior monomial).

Description
===========

Column-form analogue of *note dualPieri: dualPieri,: the $GL_n$-equivariant
projection from $\wedge^d V \otimes S_\lambda V$ onto the chosen $S_\mu V$
summand (unique up to scalar).  Construction stacks *note pieriColumn:
pieriColumn, inclusions for every addable vertical $d$-strip and inverts.
